    "Here is a Python solution using Breadth-First Search (BFS) to group equivalent states:\n\n```python\nfrom collections import deque\n\ndef group_states(grid, start, goal):\n    rows, cols = len(grid), len(grid[0])\n    directions = [(0, 1), (0, -1), (1, 0), (-1, 0)]\n    \n    # Create a visited set to keep track of visited states\n    visited = set()\n    \n    # Define the abstract states as a list of lists\n    abstract_states = []\n    \n    for i in range(rows):\n        for j in range(cols):\n            if (i, j) not in visited:\n                group = []\n                queue = deque([(i, j)])\n                visited.add((i, j))\n                \n                while queue:\n                    x, y = queue.popleft()\n                    group.append(x * cols + y)\n                    \n                    # Explore neighbors\n                    for dx, dy in directions:\n                        nx, ny = x + dx, y + dy\n                        \n                        if 0 <= nx < rows and 0 <= ny < cols and grid[nx][ny] != \"X\" and (nx, ny) not in visited:\n                            queue.append((nx, ny))\n                            visited.add((nx, ny))\n                \n                abstract_states.append(group)\n    \n    return abstract_states\n\ngrid = [[0,2,5,9,\"X\",57,49,58,62],[1,4,8,12,\"X\",50,41,51,59],[3,7,11,14,\"X\",42,36,43,52],[6,10,13,15,17,\"X\",32,\"X\",56],[\"X\",\"X\",\"X\",16,18,\"X\",27,\"X\",48],[63,60,53,\"X\",19,21,24,\"X\",40],[61,54,44,\"X\",20,23,26,31,35],[55,45,37,\"X\",22,\"X\",30,\"X\",\"X\"],[46,38,33,28,25,29,34,39,47]]\nstart = 0\ngoal = 47\n\nprint(group_states(grid, start, goal))\n```\n\nThis code uses BFS to traverse the grid and group equivalent states into abstract states. It explores neighbors in all four directions (up, down, left, right) and adds them to the current group if they are not blocked by the grid boundary or an \"X\" cell.\n\nThe output is a list of lists, where each inner list represents an abstract state containing equivalent states.\n\nNote that this implementation assumes that two states are equivalent if they have the same optimal actions and expected values when following the policy. However, since the problem statement does not provide the policy or reward structure, we can only group states based on their spatial connectivity.",
