    "Here is a Python solution to group the states based on their spatial relation and behavior:\n\n```python\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef get_abstract_states(grid):\n    rows = len(grid)\n    cols = len(grid[0])\n    \n    def is_valid(x, y):\n        return 0 <= x < rows and 0 <= y < cols\n    \n    def bfs(start_x, start_y):\n        visited = set()\n        queue = [(start_x, start_y)]\n        while queue:\n            x, y = queue.pop(0)\n            if (x, y) not in visited:\n                visited.add((x, y))\n                for dx, dy in [(-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)]:\n                    nx, ny = x + dx, y + dy\n                    if is_valid(nx, ny) and grid[nx][ny] != 'X':\n                        queue.append((nx, ny))\n        return visited\n    \n    abstract_states = defaultdict(list)\n    for i in range(rows):\n        for j in range(cols):\n            if grid[i][j] != 'X':\n                key = frozenset(bfs(i, j))\n                abstract_states[key].append(grid[i][j])\n    \n    return [list(group) for group in abstract_states.values()]\n\ngrid = [[0,2,5,9,\"X\",57,49,58,62],[1,4,8,12,\"X\",50,41,51,59],[3,7,11,14,\"X\",42,36,43,52],[6,10,13,15,17,\"X\",32,\"X\",56],[\"X\",\"X\",\"X\",16,18,\"X\",27,\"X\",48],[63,60,53,\"X\",19,21,24,\"X\",40],[61,54,44,\"X\",20,23,26,31,35],[55,45,37,\"X\",22,\"X\",30,\"X\",\"X\"],[46,38,33,28,25,29,34,39,47]]\nprint(get_abstract_states(grid))\n```\n\nThis script first checks whether a position is valid and then performs BFS to find all connected positions. The visited positions are used as keys for the abstract states dictionary. Finally, it groups the state numbers by their corresponding abstract states.\n\nPlease note that this solution may not be efficient for large grids due to its time complexity of O(n*m), where n is the number of rows and m is the number of columns in the grid.",
